- What compiler optimizations help ensure constants are folded efficiently?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests branchless bit-twiddling and cross-half reasoning on a 128-bit type. Parity is a classic low-level primitive with real-world use in checksums and error-detection.

## Key Concepts

- Branchless parity computation
- XOR folding techniques across 64-bit halves
- 128-bit struct handling without loops

## Code Example

```c
static inline unsigned parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t lo = x.lo;
  lo ^= lo >> 32;
  lo ^= lo >> 16;
  lo ^= lo >> 8;
  lo ^= lo >> 4;
  lo ^= lo >> 2;
  lo ^= lo >> 1;
  uint64_t hi = x.hi;
  hi ^= hi >> 32;
  hi ^= hi >> 16;
  hi ^= hi >> 8;
  hi ^= hi >> 4;
  hi ^= hi >> 2;
  hi ^= hi >> 1;
  return (lo & 1) ^ (hi & 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to count parity modulo any odd number (e.g., 3) with similar tricks?
- Could you prove the branchless reduction yields correct parity for all 128-bit inputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to manipulate a 128-bit value by composing 64-bit operations, enforcing a non-loop, fixed-sequence approach for performance.

## Key Concepts
- Endianness and byte-level reversal
- Bit-twiddling for 64-bit reversal
- Combining two 64-bit halves into a 128-bit result

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t v) {
  v = ((v & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8) | ((v >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
  v = ((v & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((v >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
  v = (v << 32) | (v >> 32);
  return v;
}
uint128 swapEndian128(uint128 x) {
  uint128 r;
  r.lo = reverse64(x.hi);
  r.hi = reverse64(x.lo);
  return r;
}
```

